\meta{status}{draft}
\meta{created}{2026-11-26}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-26}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} draft. The pull requests go to the branch \code{\verb~|stack/source-coverage~}, which starts from \pr{1114}. The driver flag, the runtime link, rene and the lit tools are on that branch. The two MLIR passes (\pr{1126}, \pr{1134}) and the pass wiring (\pr{1130}) are complete but are not on it yet. With all of them together, a test program gives the correct counts from end to end.}

\p{The fuzzing engine uses LLVM SanitizerCoverage. That coverage guides libFuzzer, but it does not show which lines of a Reussir source file a corpus executes. Source-based code coverage gives that report. It uses the LLVM format that clang and rustc use, and \code{\verb~|llvm-cov~} makes the report.}

\subtree[design-source-coverage-parts]{
\title{Parts}

\ol{
\li{The pass \code{\verb~|reussir-instrument-coverage~} (\pr{1126}) runs on the module that the frontend makes, before inlining and before optimization. It adds a counter at the entry of each function. It also adds a counter at the start of each region of a \code{\verb~|RegionBranchOpInterface~} op that can run a different number of times than its op (\pr{1137}), and of each closure body (\pr{1135}). A counter is a call of \code{\verb~|llvm.instrprof.increment~}. The span of a region is the union of the \code{\verb~|FileLineColRange~} locations of its ops. The pass writes the attribute \code{\verb~|reussir.coverage.map~} on each function. Conditions of \code{\verb~|scf.if~} get branch regions (\pr{1154}).}
\li{The pass \code{\verb~|reussir-emit-coverage-mapping~} (\pr{1134}) runs next. It encodes each attribute with the LLVM coverage mapping writer. It emits the \code{\verb~|__covrec_~} records and the \code{\verb~|__llvm_coverage_mapping~} header in \code{\verb~|llvm.used~}, as clang does, and it removes the attribute.}
\li{\code{\verb~|rrc --instrument-coverage~} (\pr{1116}, \pr{1130}) runs the two passes after the coverage scope is set, and lowers the counters with \code{\verb~|InstrProfilingLoweringPass~}. \pr{1151} moves the lowering to the start of the LLVM pipeline, where clang and rustc put it. rrc links \code{\verb~|profiler_builtins~} from the target libdir of the link rustc (\pr{1150}).}
\li{rene builds with coverage (\pr{1118}, \pr{1122}). \code{\verb~|rene coverage~} (\pr{1121}, \pr{1127}) runs a fuzz target over a corpus with \code{\verb~|-runs=0~}, merges the profiles with \code{\verb~|llvm-profdata~} and makes the report with \code{\verb~|llvm-cov~}. The harness writes the profile when a case crashes (\pr{1128}, \pr{1139}).}
}
}

\subtree[design-source-coverage-decisions]{
\title{Decisions}

\ul{
\li{The instrumentation runs before inlining. An inlined counter keeps the name of its function, so the count stays with the callee. The Reussir passes that stop at unknown ops skip a counter (\pr{1133}, \pr{1124}), so a coverage build optimizes like a normal build.}
\li{Each region has its own physical counter. There are no counter expressions.}
\li{A counter is the LLVM intrinsic, called through \code{\verb~|llvm.call_intrinsic~}. There is no new Reussir op.}
\li{The PGO name comes from \code{\verb~|llvm::getPGOFuncName~}: \code{\verb~|<file>:<symbol>~} for a function with local linkage. The \code{\verb~|llvm.linkage~} attribute decides this, not \code{\verb~|sym_visibility~}.}
\li{The function hash covers the counter count and each span. A changed span gives a hash mismatch, which \code{\verb~|llvm-cov~} reports. It never gives wrong counts.}
\li{The coverage scope that selects the functions for SanitizerCoverage also selects the functions for source coverage (\pr{1141}).}
\li{The runtime is \code{\verb~|profiler_builtins~}, not \code{\verb~|libclang_rt.profile~}, because Rust ships it for each target that the project builds for. The final rustc call does not get \code{\verb~|-C instrument-coverage~}, because that would add records for the temporary launcher files.}
}
}

\subtree[design-source-coverage-open]{
\title{Open questions}

\ul{
\li{Line counts: a region that starts in the middle of a line gives that line the count of the enclosing region. Gap regions (\pr{1138}, \pr{1140}) and the rule that ops with the location of the parent op do not widen a region (\pr{1162}) address this.}
\li{The span of counter 0 is the body expression, and an arm span leaves out the pattern, because MIR keeps no span for a declaration or a pattern.}
\li{A condition held in a variable gets no branch region. The right operand of \code{\verb~|&&~} and \code{\verb~|||~} gets no branch region, because it needs a counter expression or more counters.}
\li{A function that the compiler removes after instrumentation leaves a record without a name, and \code{\verb~|llvm-cov~} then rejects the binary. \pr{1168} lists such names in \code{\verb~|__llvm_coverage_names~}, as clang does. The pass must run after the last MLIR pass that removes functions, and the driver does not run it yet. The lowering must run at the start of the LLVM pipeline (\pr{1151}), or LLVM global DCE removes the names again.}
\li{A profile is lost when libFuzzer stops on a timeout, a memory limit or a call of \code{\verb~|exit~}. Continuous mode (\code{\verb~|%c~} with counter relocation) keeps it.}
\li{The span hash makes each function below an inserted line stale. Clang hashes the structure of the function. The PGO names of private functions change with \code{\verb~|--codegen-units~}.}
\li{The instrumentation pass uses \code{\verb~|reussir.coverage.map~} to find functions that already have counters, but the mapping pass removes that attribute. A second run of the instrumentation pass after the mapping pass thus adds a second set of counters.}
\li{Problems found during this work: [prob-J81O](prob-J81O), [prob-DLUF](prob-DLUF), [prob-TWW0](prob-TWW0), [prob-651X](prob-651X), [prob-SXH9](prob-SXH9).}
}
}
